# DiMaggio & Powell (1983)

**Full citation:** DiMaggio, P. J., & Powell, W. W. (1983). The iron cage revisited: Institutional isomorphism and collective rationality in organizational fields. *American Sociological Review*, *48*(2), 147–160.  
**Zotero key:** `dimaggio_powell_1983`  
**Journal:** American Sociological Review

---

## Core Argument

Organisations within the same institutional field tend toward structural similarity — isomorphism — through three mechanisms: coercive isomorphism (pressure from powerful actors), mimetic isomorphism (copying successful peers under uncertainty), and normative isomorphism (professional standards and socialisation). Critically, isomorphic pressures operate *within* institutional fields, not *across* them — organisations in different fields face different isomorphic pressures even when they govern overlapping domains.

---

## Why This Work Matters to PRIS

DiMaggio & Powell (1983) provides the **institutional theory foundation** for explaining why RBI and SEBI do not coordinate their AI governance obligations. Each regulator operates within its own institutional field (banking regulation vs capital markets regulation) and faces isomorphic pressures within that field — from the BIS/Basel framework for RBI, from IOSCO for SEBI. Neither regulator faces cross-field isomorphic pressure to coordinate with the other. This is why parallelism is structural, not accidental.

